Subject: Key native GL inventories by the Wii 32-bit handle value
Upstream-base: 11c0bc65ad2ec21431c5d74f1ac4cbc1e3e2e2c3
Classification: native ABI adaptation (integer width); no game decision changes.

GLInventory keys its model/texture/animation/skin trees by unsigned long,
which is 32 bits on the Wii and wider natively. All keys are 32-bit hash
handles, but natively an int hash passed as a key sign-extends while a
32-bit handle field zero-extends: AsyncImage adds its portrait texture as
0xffffffff8bf3fb08 and later replaces it through the texture resource's
0x8bf3fb08, so glx_GetTex returns null and glplatTextureReplace faults
(Domination sidekick page). Key every tree by the low 32 bits in native
builds; each console statement is unchanged.

diff --git a/src/Game/GL/GLInventory.cpp b/src/Game/GL/GLInventory.cpp
--- a/src/Game/GL/GLInventory.cpp
+++ b/src/Game/GL/GLInventory.cpp
@@ -16,6 +16,17 @@
 
 #include <string.h>
 
+#if defined(MSCHARGED_NATIVE)
+// Inventory keys are Wii 32-bit hash handles. Native unsigned long is wider:
+// an int hash passed as a key sign-extends while a 32-bit handle field
+// zero-extends. Key every tree by the low 32 bits, as the Wii's 32-bit
+// unsigned long does, so both name the same item.
+static inline unsigned long NativeInventoryKey(unsigned long key)
+{
+    return static_cast<unsigned int>(key);
+}
+#endif
